# Caddyfile for the developer-documentation container (profile: backend-docs).
#
# WHY CADDY RATHER THAN NGINX
#   The gateway already protects loki/prometheus/redis-insight with Caddy's
#   `basicauth` and a bcrypt hash. Reusing the same server here means one hash
#   format, one generation command, and no new image — the stack already pulls
#   caddy:2.7-alpine. nginx would need an .htpasswd file in a hash format the
#   official image ships no tooling to produce.
#
# WHY THE AUTH LIVES HERE AND NOT ONLY IN THE GATEWAY
#   This container PUBLISHES ITS OWN HOST PORT (DOCS_PORT, default 8088), so
#   traffic to it never passes through Caddy's gateway routes. A `basicauth`
#   block in docker-config/Caddyfile protects docs.<domain> and nothing else —
#   anyone who can reach the published port would still read every page. The
#   gateway route exists too, but this file is what actually closes 8088.
#
# FAILS CLOSED
#   Caddy refuses to start if DOCS_BASIC_AUTH_PASSWORD_HASH is missing or is not
#   a valid bcrypt hash. That is deliberate: a documentation portal that silently
#   comes up unauthenticated is the failure this file exists to prevent.
#
# ESCAPE THE HASH IN .env  —  the single most likely misconfiguration
#   A bcrypt hash is full of "$", and docker compose treats a single "$" as
#   variable interpolation. Every "$" must be DOUBLED in .env:
#
#       generated by caddy :  $2b$12$AYiniJ1vUP07vGD...
#       written in .env    :  $$2b$$12$$AYiniJ1vUP07vGD...
#       seen by this file  :  $2b$12$AYiniJ1vUP07vGD...
#
#   Compose collapses "$$" back to "$" on the way in, so the doubled form is the
#   correct one to store. Get this wrong and Caddy sees a malformed hash and the
#   container will not start — which is the good outcome, since the alternative
#   is a password nobody can guess and nobody intended.

{
	# No admin API inside this container; it has nothing to administer and the
	# endpoint would be reachable from the compose network.
	admin off
	# The site address below is plain :80. Certificates terminate at the gateway.
	auto_https off
}

:80 {
	root * /usr/share/caddy

	basicauth {
		{$DOCS_BASIC_AUTH_USER} {$DOCS_BASIC_AUTH_PASSWORD_HASH}
	}

	encode zstd gzip

	header {
		X-Frame-Options "DENY"
		X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
		Referrer-Policy "strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
		# Docsify pulls its runtime from jsdelivr, so script-src cannot be 'self'.
		#
		# The jsdelivr entries are pinned to https:. That makes every <script> and
		# <link> in docs/index.html REQUIRE an explicit "https://" — a
		# protocol-relative "//cdn.jsdelivr.net/..." resolves to http:// when this
		# portal is served over plain HTTP (which it is, on DOCS_PORT), and an
		# http:// URL does not match an https: source expression. The result is
		# every script silently blocked and the page stuck on
		# "Loading Developer Portal...". Keep the URLs absolute and https.
		Content-Security-Policy "default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://cdn.jsdelivr.net; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://cdn.jsdelivr.net; font-src 'self' data: https://cdn.jsdelivr.net; img-src 'self' data:; connect-src 'self'"
		-Server
	}

	# Docsify routes on the URL hash, so no SPA rewrite is needed — every request
	# is for a real file that exists on disk.
	file_server
}
